                <pb facs="0793" n="661" sameAs="frus:frus1926v01/pg661"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7953: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-12-17T11:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 17,
                                1926—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 17—4:28
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>617. My 615, December 15, 3 p.m. Following from American consul general at
                    Shanghai: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“December 16, 3 p.m. Southern vanguard entered Hangchow yesterday morning
                            and with the assistance of Chekiang officers forced Civil Governor Ch’en
                            Yi to abandon office. Southern or allied Chekiang forces have cut
                            railway and established themselves south of Linping, while Marshal Sun
                            and vanguard have reached Changan and hostilities are imminent. Heavy
                            Southern reinforcements are already moving from western Chekiang.
                            Shanghai outwardly quiet at the moment.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
